  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1015 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nMostly quiet, dry, and warmer conditions are expected for the first \nhalf of the extended forecast package as the FA finds itself between \nsystems as an omega block like pattern sets up across the CONUS. As \nthe shortwave trough from earlier this week translates towards the \nGreat Lakes, a stronger and more defined closed low and longwave \ntrough will dig into much of the western CONUS. As a result, \nsouthwest flow aloft will continue across the region while at the \nsurface, lee cyclogenesis will aid in prevailing southerly surface \nwinds. This southerly flow from the surface to H7 level will work to \nincrease thickness values across the region, which despite near \nstationary geopotential heights will allow from a modest warming \ntrend through the early weekend. Expect highs to range in the 60s \nThursday through Saturday as we expect early morning stratus to \nerode quickly with afternoon partly clear to mostly clear skies \nallowing for diurnal heating to take place. The synoptic pattern \nbegins to change by the end of the weekend, with both ensemble and \ndeterministic guidance swinging a cold front across the region as \nthe long wave trough over the western CONUS breaks down as it moves \ninto the Southern Plains. Global NWP still differs in regards to the \nevolution and timing of this system, with the ECMWF showing a more \nnortherly ejection of the trough while the GFS is more southerly. \nDespite this, both models keep our FA relatively dry with the bulk \nof the monsoonal/subtropical moisture from the H3 to H7 layer \ndisplaced to our east. As a result, PoPs remain near zero across the \narea, though as model guidance becomes more aligned in the coming \ndays, this may need to be altered for areas across the eastern \nRolling Plains. \n\nAlthough confidence in precipitation remains low with this system, \nmodels are in agreement with the potential for a modest cool down \nwith DESI probabilities suggesting afternoon high temperatures may \nstruggle to break out of the 40s and 50s Sunday through Tuesday, \nwhile overnight lows drop right at or just below freezing as the \nenvironment struggles to mix out. Additionally, as our next \npotential system moves into the region from the west, ensembles \nhighlight a potential for the return of precipitation. However, \nconsidering this is still several days out this remains low \nconfidence and will maintain NBM mentionable PoPs for the time \nbeing.",
